RETRACTION. An earlier record in this note called the oct_torsor_act
composition order a shipped DEFECT. That was an OVERCLAIM and is withdrawn.
tests/test_oct_torsor_rc388.py::test_ratchet2_action_law_and_naive_defect
already measures it, already asserts the REVERSED order (t<|g)<|h == t<|(h.g)
at 14336/14336, and already pins the naive order at 8960/14336 over all 28
seams — its own docstring names the trap: "the naive composition order still
LANDS IN T while being the WRONG element". The op's docstring also contains
the fact, if understated: "R_g == L_{conj g} on T" IS the anti-homomorphism
statement, since a right multiplication equal to a left multiplication
reverses composition order.

What this section actually delivers is an INDEPENDENT reproduction of that
ratchet from a different direction — interval composition rather than action
composition — landing on the identical 5/8 (our single seam 320/512, the
shipped ratchet 8960/14336). That is evidence the harness measures the
carrier and not itself.

CITATIONS. Adds a citations record; every entry verified by fetching an
accessible source. Gustafson's 5/8 (Amer. Math. Monthly 80:9 (1973)
1031-1034) is paywalled, so arXiv:2010.01188 and groupprops are named as the
OA corroborators actually fetched. Hille-Yosida / Lumer-Phillips / the group
criterion come from Buehler & Salamon's ETH lecture notes (8 June 2017),
Theorems 7.21 / 7.28 and Definition 7.27.

Two corrections worth carrying:

* Baez, "The Octonions" (arXiv:math/0105155) does NOT contain the string
  "Moufang" — it covers alternativity only. The Moufang-loop-of-order-16
  claim needs Wikipedia + Schafer (Academic Press 1966, Ch. III, eqns 7-9,
  public domain at Project Gutenberg #25156) instead.
* (ab)^-1 = b^-1 a^-1 on the octonion loop could NOT be verified from any
  reachable source with a theorem number. It is therefore DERIVED-and-
  MEASURED here rather than cited: 256/256, cross-checked against the
  independent exact-Q cd_mult construction. That is precisely the claim the
  brief flagged as "should still work" — so it is now measured rather than
  assumed.

